<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/expert/demography">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Demography is <strong>the mathematics of populations — births, deaths, migration — and the
    social science with the most reliable forecasts</strong> (everyone who will be 40 in 2060 is
    already born). Its master pattern (the demographic transition) explains the population boom,
    the ageing of nations, and the century's biggest open question.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the transition, momentum, age structures, the second transition" widget="equationStep" config={demogCfg} />

  <h2>Demographic facts with consequences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Life expectancy doubled since 1850:</strong> mostly public health, not medicine (the medicine lesson's finding) — the greatest statistical achievement in human history, mostly plumbing and vaccines.</li>
    <li><strong>Fertility is falling almost everywhere:</strong> over half of countries below replacement (2.1); South Korea at ~0.7 — the lowest ever recorded. Pronatalist policies have weak measured effects; the drivers (women's education, urbanisation, cost) are structural.</li>
    <li><strong>Population ageing is the fiscal event of the century:</strong> old-age dependency ratios double by 2050 in most rich countries — pension and health systems designed for 4 workers per retiree meet 2. Every political fight about budgets is partly this curve.</li>
    <li><strong>Migration is the wildcard:</strong> the only component that can change fast — and the politics of it run hotter than the arithmetic (the geography lesson's migration data, sociologically priced).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UN World Population Prospects (2024); Lee, "The Demographic Transition" (2003); Lutz, World Population and Human Capital in the 21st Century.</p>
</TopicPage>
